The training set shows that large spillage co-occurs with SOC-driven inversion and is almost always realized in cubic (half-)Heusler, inverse-perovskite or related stuffed-zincblende structures that contain a heavy p-block anion. The sole example that simultaneously satisfies the gap threshold is the 8-electron half-Heusler LiMgBi (spillage 2.02, gap 1.11 eV). All other high-spillage entries are metals or have gaps well below 1 eV.

Consequently the pool was screened first for the same 8-electron count and the same Wyckoff pattern (atoms at 0, ¼, ½), then for the heaviest p-block elements that still keep the compound on the insulating side of the Zintl line. This yields five candidates whose inferred gaps remain \(\ge1\) eV while their SOC strength (and therefore expected spillage) is maximal:

- c007 (CdCuSb) is the direct 8-electron half-Heusler analogue of LiMgBi;
- c048 (BiTe2Br4) supplies the heaviest p-block combination (Bi/Te/Br) in a low-dimensional but still gapped packing;
- c022 (PtS2) is the 1T dichalcogenide with the strongest 5d SOC that remains a semiconductor;
- c057 (cubic Li3Sb) is the 8-electron DO3 Zintl phase whose gap is known to stay above 1 eV;
- c097 (PdI2) is the iodide analogue of the high-spillage hexahalides already present in training.

These five are therefore ranked by decreasing expected spillage.

Final: c007, c048, c022, c057, c097
